    "K_t": "Evidence:\n[1] In 2011, Grover Babcock and Blue Hadaegh were looking for an example of a coerced confession as the basis for a documentary film they were planning and they used Thomas' interrogation video to make the documentary \"Scenes of a Crime\".\n[2] Believed to have committed 17 killings as a hired gunman in the West, in 1902 Horn was convicted of the murder of 14-year-old Willie Nickell near Iron Mountain, Wyoming.\n[3] William Brydges was a Canadian man whose arrest for murder resulted in the leading Supreme Court of Canada case \"R v Brydges\" on the right of a detainee to access duty counsel.\n[4] He is best known for the arrest of gunman Tom Horn in 1903 for the alleged murder of 14‑year-old sheepherder Willie Nickell, which has since come into question, and it has long been believed that Lefors falsified evidence helping to convict the wrong man for the murder."
